ed  nations and consulting on the undp um he  was also information advisor to uh  indian prime minister vp singh um and  was a former editor of and the contr  contributor to the hindu newspaper but  also has written for many many indian  publications so there we go a really  distinguished panel to discuss things  with us um let's start by  opening with a pitch from all our  speakers which looks at the central  question when it comes to trade  which goal is more important praveen  krishna  is it greater prosperity  or is it reducing inequality  um on the issue of international trade  uh let me say the following  international trade first of all has  been a key driver of growth and poverty  reduction parts of your question about  inequality i want to be careful in my  comments to distinguish between poverty  and poverty reduction which is which are  some kind of one end of the income  distribution that  essentially what happens at the bottom  of the income distribution and  rather than the difference as the rich  getting richer while the poor may also  be getting richer and then what's the  difference between the two these are  these are distinct concepts and i think  they need to be treated uh separately  but first as far as just international  trade and  being over the reduction  let's keep in mind the example of china  a stunning example of a country which  has been able to draw hundreds of  millions of its citizens out of poverty  with a growth model that was largely  based on technological change and  international and much the same is true  in india where we also have seen large  reductions in poverty in the two decades  after india began its market reform so  poverty in india maybe about 50 percent  of the 1970s early 1980s is down to  about 20 it's a very significant  reduction  in the context of international trade  once again in market reforms  and indeed no developing country one  could argue is achieved kind of high in  sustained levels of growth without  