s best  but this  this competitive market is itself a  sort of a passing thing  because the competition itself wipes out  the weather  and the numbers left become smaller and  smaller and eventually if you look at  all the the the mature capitalist  economies of today they are oligopolies  there is just no free market  that's the honest truth  the fact is the free market today is a  myth  what is free  however is  that  in this  in in the in the domestic markets you  have the capacity the state has the  capacity and the workers have the  capacity to in fact make sure that the  benefits from the increased productivity  and efficiency of competition do get  passed on to the to the to the workers  and you should britain is an excellent  example i don't i don't need to go any  further but the whole whole of  continental europe and to considerable  extent the us are all in this category  free trade is between countries and that  today  is a complete free for all  and that does not necessarily lead to  greater prosperity in in in in the  poorer countries yes we have  one last point  yes it is true that's the east asia  um and southeast asia benefited from  free trade but they benefited even more  from the transfer of technology  to these countries in order to enable  them to be able to  trade in the goods that the west needed  okay thank you very much um and last but  not least michael howard what's your  pitch  i think we're really  trying to build a conflict where it  doesn't exist there's no necessarily  conflict between prosperity and equality  um as has been pointed out as praveen  pointed out  free trade uh and capitalism  have in the last quarter of a century  led to the greatest reduction in poverty  our planet has ever seen uh and we don't  celebrate it enough uh and that couldn't  have happened without capitalism and it  couldn't have happened without a a  considerable degree of free trade  because the countries  in which uh the greatest reduction in  poverty ha